    question: 'A 26-week premature neonate on HFOV develops sudden desaturation to 70%, bilateral chest rise is absent on the right, and transillumination is positive on the right side. What is the immediate priority?',
    answer: 'The immediate priority is needle decompression of a right-sided tension pneumothorax. Insert an 18- to 20-gauge angiocatheter at the second or third intercostal space at the midclavicular line, or at the fourth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line. After decompression, place a chest tube for ongoing drainage. On HFOV, air leaks are a known complication because of continuous distending pressure. Positive transillumination in a premature infant with acute deterioration is highly suggestive of pneumothorax, and treatment should not be delayed for a confirmatory X-ray when clinical signs are compelling.',
