    answer: 'On a supine AP chest X-ray (the standard positioning for neonates), free air collects anteriorly and medially rather than at the apex. Findings include a hyperlucent hemithorax (the affected side appears darker than the contralateral side), a deep costophrenic sulcus sign (deep sulcus sign) where the sulcus extends unusually far inferiorly, a sharp cardiac border on the affected side due to air outlining the heart, and visible anterior pleural reflection. A large pneumothorax causes mediastinal shift with the heart and trachea pushed away from the affected side. On a traditional upright film, air collects at the apex and is more readily identified as a visible visceral pleural line. Cross-table lateral or lateral decubitus films with the affected side up can help confirm an equivocal supine pneumothorax.',
