    answer: 'Classic BPD progresses through recognized stages on chest X-ray. Stage I (days 1 to 3) resembles RDS with diffuse granularity. Stage II (days 4 to 10) shows increasing opacification as lung injury progresses. Stage III (days 10 to 20) demonstrates alternating areas of atelectasis and cystic lucencies, giving a bubbly or sponge-like appearance. Stage IV (beyond day 28) shows coarse interstitial markings, hyperinflation with flattened diaphragms, cystic areas interspersed with fibrotic strands, and cardiomegaly from developing cor pulmonale. The new form of BPD seen in extremely premature infants often shows diffuse haziness without the dramatic cystic changes, reflecting arrested alveolar development rather than the classic fibroproliferative pattern.',
