    answer: 'Neonatal cranial ultrasound is performed through the anterior fontanelle and is the primary imaging modality for detecting IVH in premature infants. The Papile grading system classifies IVH into four grades: Grade I is isolated germinal matrix hemorrhage (subependymal hemorrhage) without intraventricular extension. Grade II is IVH with blood filling less than 50% of the lateral ventricle without ventricular dilation. Grade III is IVH with blood filling greater than 50% of the lateral ventricle with ventricular dilation (distension). Grade IV, now more accurately called periventricular hemorrhagic infarction (PVHI), shows intraparenchymal echodensity adjacent to the ventricle from venous infarction. Grades III and IV carry the highest risk of neurodevelopmental impairment.',
