    question: 'What radiographic finding on chest X-ray is pathognomonic for congenital diaphragmatic hernia?',
    answer: 'The presence of bowel gas patterns (air-filled loops of intestine) within the thoracic cavity is pathognomonic for congenital diaphragmatic hernia. On a left-sided CDH (which comprises approximately 85% of cases), the X-ray shows multiple air-filled and fluid-filled loops in the left hemithorax, displacement of the mediastinum and heart to the right, an absent or indistinct left diaphragmatic outline, and a relatively gasless abdomen due to bowel displacement into the chest. The nasogastric tube may be seen curling into the chest if the stomach has herniated. On the initial X-ray before the infant has swallowed air, the herniated bowel may appear as a homogeneous opacity mimicking a pleural effusion or consolidation, making early films potentially misleading.',
