    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'How does periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) appear on neonatal cranial ultrasound, and what is its significance?',
    answer: 'Periventricular leukomalacia initially appears on cranial ultrasound as bilateral, symmetric periventricular echodensities (increased echogenicity) in the white matter adjacent to the lateral ventricles, most prominent in the frontal and parieto-occipital regions. Over 2 to 4 weeks, these echodensities may evolve into periventricular cystic lesions (cystic PVL), which appear as anechoic (dark) cavities within the previously echogenic areas. Eventually, the cysts may collapse, and the ventricles enlarge into the areas of tissue loss (ex vacuo ventriculomegaly). PVL is the most common cause of cerebral palsy in premature infants. It results from ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter, which is a vascular watershed zone particularly vulnerable in preterm infants between 24 and 34 weeks of gestation.',
