    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What are the chest X-ray findings of neonatal pleural effusion, and what are the common causes in neonates?',
    answer: 'On a supine neonatal chest X-ray, pleural effusion appears as a uniform increase in density over the affected hemithorax with a hazy ground glass quality, sometimes with a visible meniscus sign at the lateral chest wall. Fluid may track into the interlobar fissures. Large effusions cause mediastinal shift away from the affected side. A lateral decubitus film (affected side down) can confirm the diagnosis by showing layering of the fluid. Common causes in neonates include chylothorax (most common spontaneous effusion in neonates, resulting from thoracic duct injury or lymphatic malformation), hydrops fetalis (immune or nonimmune), congenital heart failure, neonatal pneumonia with parapneumonic effusion, and iatrogenic causes such as central line extravasation of TPN.',
