    question: 'How does Pierre Robin sequence affect neonatal airway management?',
    answer: 'Pierre Robin sequence consists of micrognathia (small mandible), glossoptosis (posterior displacement of the tongue), and a U-shaped cleft palate. This triad creates significant upper airway obstruction because the tongue falls posteriorly into the pharynx, particularly in the supine position. Airway management includes prone positioning as the first-line intervention to allow gravity to pull the tongue forward, nasopharyngeal airway placement to stent open the posterior pharynx, and CPAP or high-flow nasal cannula for moderate obstruction. Intubation is often extremely difficult due to the recessed mandible obscuring the laryngeal view. Video laryngoscopy, fiberoptic intubation, or LMA placement may be necessary. In severe cases refractory to conservative management, mandibular distraction osteogenesis or tongue-lip adhesion surgery may be required. Feeding difficulties coexist and require specialized support.',
