    question: 'How does spinal muscular atrophy type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease) affect neonatal respiratory function?',
    answer: 'SMA type I is an autosomal recessive neuromuscular disease caused by deletions in the SMN1 gene, resulting in progressive degeneration of anterior horn cells in the spinal cord. Affected neonates present with severe generalized hypotonia, absent deep tendon reflexes, fasciculations of the tongue, weak cry, paradoxical (seesaw) breathing pattern from intercostal weakness with relatively preserved diaphragm function, and progressive respiratory failure. The bell-shaped chest deformity develops from intercostal muscle weakness. Respiratory management historically involved supportive care with noninvasive ventilation, cough-assist devices, and airway clearance techniques. The landscape has changed dramatically with disease-modifying therapies including nusinersen (intrathecal antisense oligonucleotide), onasemnogene abeparvovec (gene replacement therapy), and risdiplam (oral SMN2 splicing modifier), which can significantly improve motor function and survival when initiated early.',
