    answer: 'Congenital myotonic dystrophy (CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene, inherited from an affected mother through anticipation) presents with severe neonatal hypotonia, facial diplegia (tented upper lip), respiratory insufficiency, arthrogryposis (joint contractures), and feeding difficulties. Respiratory failure is the primary cause of neonatal mortality, occurring in up to 25% of severely affected infants. Respiratory challenges include diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle weakness requiring prolonged mechanical ventilation (sometimes weeks to months), difficult extubation due to pharyngeal and laryngeal weakness, high aspiration risk from bulbar dysfunction, and elevated diaphragms from muscular weakness. If the infant survives the neonatal period, respiratory function typically improves over months as muscle strength gradually increases. Genetic testing of the mother is diagnostic, and the severity correlates with the number of CTG repeats.',
