    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'How does Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II) present in the neonatal period with respect to respiratory function?',
    answer: 'Infantile-onset Pompe disease is caused by deficiency of acid alpha-glucosidase (GAA), leading to glycogen accumulation in cardiac and skeletal muscle. Neonates present with profound hypotonia, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (massive cardiomegaly visible on chest X-ray), macroglossia, feeding difficulties, and progressive respiratory failure. The respiratory failure results from both skeletal respiratory muscle weakness (diaphragm and intercostal muscles) and cardiac failure from the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Chest X-ray shows a massively enlarged cardiac silhouette with pulmonary congestion. Without treatment, death from cardiorespiratory failure typically occurs by 1 year of age. Enzyme replacement therapy with alglucosidase alfa has significantly improved survival and cardiac function when initiated early. Newborn screening has been implemented in many states to enable presymptomatic diagnosis and early treatment.',
