    question: 'What is the respiratory significance of DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome) in neonates?',
    answer: 'DiGeorge syndrome results from a microdeletion on chromosome 22q11.2 and affects structures derived from the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. Respiratory implications include congenital heart defects (present in approximately 75% of cases, commonly interrupted aortic arch, truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot, and VSD), airway anomalies including vascular rings, aberrant subclavian artery causing tracheal or esophageal compression, laryngeal web, and subglottic stenosis. Absent or hypoplastic thymus on chest X-ray is a hallmark finding. Hypocalcemia from parathyroid hypoplasia can cause laryngospasm and apnea. T-cell immunodeficiency increases susceptibility to respiratory infections. If blood products are needed, only irradiated and CMV-negative products should be used due to the immunodeficiency. Velopharyngeal insufficiency may contribute to feeding and airway issues.',
