    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What is near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS), and how is it used in neonatal monitoring?',
    answer: 'Near-infrared spectroscopy is a noninvasive technology that uses near-infrared light (wavelengths 700 to 1000 nm) to continuously measure regional tissue oxygen saturation (rSO2) in real time. In the NICU, sensors are placed on the forehead to measure cerebral oxygen saturation (cerebral rSO2, normal range approximately 55 to 85%) and on the flank or abdomen to measure somatic (renal or splanchnic) oxygen saturation. NIRS provides information about the balance between tissue oxygen delivery and consumption. Low cerebral rSO2 may indicate decreased cerebral blood flow, hypotension, anemia, or hypoxemia. A widening cerebral-somatic rSO2 gap may indicate impaired splanchnic perfusion, as seen in NEC or ductal steal. NIRS trends are more clinically useful than absolute values and guide interventions such as transfusion thresholds and hemodynamic optimization.',
