    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What is the role of end-tidal CO2 (ETCO2) monitoring in neonatal intubation and mechanical ventilation?',
    answer: 'End-tidal CO2 monitoring serves two primary roles in neonatal care. First, during intubation, the detection of exhaled CO2 (colorimetric or capnographic) is the most reliable method for confirming endotracheal tube placement in the trachea versus the esophagus. The American Heart Association and NRP guidelines recommend ETCO2 detection as the primary confirmation method. Second, during ongoing mechanical ventilation, continuous capnography provides real-time trending of ventilation adequacy. In neonates, ETCO2 typically underestimates PaCO2 by 2 to 5 mmHg due to higher dead space-to-tidal volume ratios and ventilation-perfusion mismatch. The gradient between PaCO2 and ETCO2 widens with worsening lung disease, increased dead space (as in BPD), or low cardiac output. Mainstream sensors are preferred in neonates over sidestream sensors because sidestream sampling can entrain a significant portion of the small tidal volume.',
