    question: 'What is electrical impedance tomography (EIT), and how might it be applied in neonatal ventilation management?',
    answer: 'Electrical impedance tomography is an emerging noninvasive imaging technique that uses a belt of electrodes placed around the chest to measure regional changes in thoracic impedance during ventilation. Because air has high impedance and fluid/tissue has low impedance, EIT creates real-time cross-sectional images of ventilation distribution within the lungs. In neonatal care, potential applications include assessing regional lung aeration to identify areas of overdistension or atelectasis, titrating PEEP to achieve optimal lung recruitment by finding the level that produces the most homogeneous ventilation distribution, monitoring the effects of surfactant administration, detecting pneumothorax or pleural effusion, and evaluating patient-ventilator synchrony. EIT is radiation-free, continuous, and bedside-applicable. Limitations include lower spatial resolution than CT or MRI and sensitivity to electrode placement and body position changes.',
