    divisionId: NPS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'A term neonate with respiratory distress has an SpO2 of 75% on room air. The hyperoxia test results in SpO2 increasing to only 78% on 100% FiO2. Chest X-ray shows decreased pulmonary vascularity. What is the clinical interpretation, and what are the next steps?',
    answer: 'A failed hyperoxia test (SpO2 remaining below 85% or PaO2 below 100 mmHg on 100% FiO2) in the setting of decreased pulmonary vascularity strongly suggests cyanotic congenital heart disease with ductal-dependent pulmonary blood flow, such as pulmonary atresia, critical pulmonary stenosis, tricuspid atresia, or severe tetralogy of Fallot. The next steps are to immediately start prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil) infusion at 0.05 to 0.1 mcg/kg/min to maintain ductal patency and ensure pulmonary blood flow, obtain an urgent echocardiogram to define the cardiac anatomy, consult pediatric cardiology and cardiac surgery, establish secure intravenous access, monitor for PGE1 side effects (apnea in up to 12% requiring intubation readiness, hypotension, fever), and avoid excessive FiO2 which provides no benefit in fixed cardiac shunting. Do not delay PGE1 initiation while awaiting the echocardiogram.',
